const positions=`SELECT pg_last_wal_receive_lsn() AS received,
       pg_last_wal_replay_lsn() AS replayed,
       pg_get_wal_replay_pause_state() AS replay_state;
SELECT product_id, price FROM prices;`;
const writer=`UPDATE prices SET price = 25 WHERE product_id = 7;
-- Run after the UPDATE has committed in this autocommit session.
SELECT pg_current_wal_lsn() AS marker;`;
const monitor=`SELECT application_name, state, sync_state,
       sent_lsn, write_lsn, flush_lsn, replay_lsn
FROM pg_stat_replication;
SELECT slot_name, active, restart_lsn, wal_status,
       pg_wal_lsn_diff(pg_current_wal_lsn(), restart_lsn)
         AS retained_distance_bytes
FROM pg_replication_slots;`;
const publication=`-- Publisher: an existing orders table has this primary key:
-- PRIMARY KEY (tenant_id, order_id)
CREATE PUBLICATION tenant_seven FOR TABLE orders
WHERE (tenant_id = 7);`;
const setup=`# PostgreSQL 18 binaries must be on PATH. Use an ordinary OS account.
LAB=$(mktemp -d /tmp/pg-replication.XXXXXX)
mkdir "$LAB/socket"
initdb -D "$LAB/a" -A trust -U lab --no-locale -E UTF8
pg_ctl -D "$LAB/a" -l "$LAB/a.log" -w start -o \
 "-c listen_addresses='' -c unix_socket_directories=$LAB/socket -p 55439 -c wal_keep_size=128MB"
psql -h "$LAB/socket" -p 55439 -U lab -d postgres -c \
 'CREATE TABLE prices(product_id integer PRIMARY KEY, price integer NOT NULL); INSERT INTO prices VALUES(7,20);'
pg_basebackup -D "$LAB/b" -R -X stream -C -S scale_b \
 -d "host=$LAB/socket port=55439 user=lab application_name=scale_b"
pg_ctl -D "$LAB/b" -l "$LAB/b.log" -w start -o \
 "-c listen_addresses='' -c unix_socket_directories=$LAB/socket -p 55440 -c wal_keep_size=128MB"
# Open these in separate terminals, using the printed LAB path:
echo "$LAB"
psql -h "$LAB/socket" -p 55439 -U lab -d postgres   # A
# psql -h "$LAB/socket" -p 55440 -U lab -d postgres # B`;
---
<Base title="Following a change to another PostgreSQL server" description="Trace WAL send, flush and replay, then work through read routing, logical replication, slot retention and a tested promotion and rejoin.">
<article class="visual-essay relational-design">
 <header class="essay-header"><p class="eyebrow">Databases and storage</p><h1>Following a change to another PostgreSQL server</h1><TitleLine type="PostgreSQL deep dive" version="PostgreSQL 18"><a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/architecture/">Inside PostgreSQL ↗</a></TitleLine></header>
<ArticleBody>
 <p>A second PostgreSQL server can hold the log record for a new price and still return the old price to a query. Receiving a change and making that change visible are separate pieces of work. When we move reads away from the writer, the gap between them becomes part of the application's behaviour.</p>
 <p>PostgreSQL's physical replication extends its recovery machinery across servers. The primary sends write-ahead log records; the standby stores and replays them into its own copy of the database. Logical replication takes a different path, decoding table changes and applying them to independently stored tables. We will follow both paths, then promote a standby and bring the former primary back onto the chosen history.</p>
 <p>The physical experiments below ran on two isolated PostgreSQL 18.6 servers with ordinary logged tables and the default durable local commit settings. A is the primary, B its streaming standby. Both initially contain product 7 at price 20. These are server observations; the browser sandbox cannot run these background processes or demonstrate failover.</p>

 <section id="physical"><h2>Physical replication: receive, flush and replay</h2>
  <p>A physical standby needs a starting copy whose data files and recovery information fit together. <code>pg_basebackup</code> creates that copy; streaming WAL then carries it forward. On A, a <strong>WAL sender</strong> reads the stream. On B, a <strong>WAL receiver</strong> writes it locally, while the startup process performs recovery by replaying records. A running standby can accept read-only connections while remaining in recovery.</p>
  <p>Positions in this stream are <strong>log sequence numbers</strong>, or LSNs. PostgreSQL prints them as two hexadecimal parts separated by a slash. Treat one as a byte position, not a transaction number: many records can belong to one transaction, and some describe work that ultimately rolls back. The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/recovery/">recovery article</a> follows how commit records and page changes fit together.</p>
  <details><summary>Set up two disposable servers for the experiment</summary><p>This local exercise needs PostgreSQL 18 command-line tools and two unused ports, 55439 and 55440. The servers listen only on Unix sockets. Trust authentication is limited to this disposable local setup. Keep the shell variable <code>LAB</code> in the setup terminal; copy its printed path into the second terminal. If either start command fails, inspect its log before continuing.</p><pre><code>{setup}</code></pre><p>Confirm <code>SELECT pg_is_in_recovery();</code> returns false on A and true on B, and that both read price 20. Leave each SQL session in autocommit mode. At the end, exit psql and stop both servers from the setup shell with <code>pg_ctl -D "$LAB/b" -m fast -w stop</code> and <code>pg_ctl -D "$LAB/a" -m fast -w stop</code>. Their directories remain available for inspection; remove only this disposable directory when finished.</p></details>
  <p>First, an operator connected to B runs <code>SELECT pg_wal_replay_pause();</code> and waits until <code>pg_get_wal_replay_pause_state()</code> reports <code>paused</code>. This suspends replay while allowing receipt of WAL. In A's separate autocommit session, change the price and capture a position after the commit:</p>
  <Sql code={writer} label="On primary A: commit price 25, then capture a marker" />
  <p>The marker can include other work that reached the shared log before the SELECT. That makes it a conservative position to wait for, rather than an exact identifier for this one transaction. Now inspect B:</p>
  <Sql code={positions} label="On standby B: compare receipt, replay and the visible row" />
  <Diagram kind="physical" />
  <p>In the checked run, A's marker was <code>0/3004BD8</code>. B had received through that position, but its replay position remained <code>0/3000000</code> and its price remained 20. The exact LSNs vary between runs. The important observation is that receipt reached the marker while replay did not.</p>
  <p>Run <code>SELECT pg_wal_replay_resume();</code> on B. Wait until its replay position reaches your marker, comparing values as the <code>pg_lsn</code> type, then issue a new SELECT. Our new read returned 25. Replay had processed the required records, including commit, so a new snapshot could see the changed row. PostgreSQL documents these functions in its <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/functions-admin.html#FUNCTIONS-RECOVERY-CONTROL">recovery controls</a>.</p>
 </section>

 <section id="reads"><h2>Read freshness and synchronous replication</h2>
  <p>Suppose a customer changes a price, receives success, then immediately reloads the product page. Sending that second request to any healthy standby can still return the old value. A successful connection says nothing about how far that standby has replayed.</p>
  <p>One application policy sends this customer's dependent read to the writer. Another carries a progress marker from the completed write, waits for a particular standby to replay through it, and only then starts the read's snapshot. A timeout needs a deliberate branch: use the writer, wait longer within the request budget, or report that the required freshness is unavailable. An already-open Repeatable Read transaction can retain its older snapshot even after the standby catches up. The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=replica-freshness">browser freshness lesson</a> lets you vary copied and applied progress; it is an explicit table model, while the server experiment above observes real WAL replay.</p>
  <p>The position check also belongs to a particular history. After failover, an LSN alone does not establish that a different server contains the same committed operations. A router needs to know which primary history and replica the marker describes; it cannot turn arbitrary numbers from different clusters into a freshness guarantee.</p>
  <p>Synchronous replication can move some waiting into COMMIT. It requires a selected standby as well as a commit policy. In our isolated A, we set <code>synchronous_standby_names = 'FIRST 1 (scale_b)'</code>, where <code>scale_b</code> is B's replication connection name, and verified B's <code>sync_state</code> became <code>sync</code>. We then paused B's replay again.</p>
  <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Observed commits with standby replay paused"><table><thead><tr><th>A's session policy</th><th>Write on A</th><th>What happened before replay resumed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>synchronous_commit=on</code></td><td>price 26</td><td>Success returned after B flushed the required WAL. B still read 25.</td></tr><tr><td><code>synchronous_commit=remote_apply</code></td><td>price 27</td><td>The backend waited on <code>SyncRep</code>. No success reply yet.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
  <p>Resuming replay allowed the second commit to finish; a new read on B returned 27. <code>remote_apply</code> waits for replay on the standby or standbys that satisfy the configured synchronous policy. It does not make every other replica current. With a quorum policy, the subset satisfying a commit can vary. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-wal.html#GUC-SYNCHRONOUS-COMMIT">commit settings</a> also distinguish remote write from durable remote flush.</p>
  <p>This wait has an operating cost. A slow or unavailable required standby can hold commits and their locks, so requests unrelated to the customer's read may wait too. Cancelling a client that is waiting for synchronous replication does not safely establish that its locally committed transaction rolled back. The caller still needs a stable operation identity to resolve an uncertain outcome.</p>
  <p>Long standby queries create another choice. The primary can vacuum away row versions that an old standby snapshot still needs. When replay reaches that cleanup, B must delay replay or cancel the query. <code>max_standby_streaming_delay</code> limits waiting over received WAL, rather than granting each query a fresh full timeout. <code>hot_standby_feedback</code> can ask the primary to retain needed versions, making its cleanup and storage pay for the report. It does not eliminate every replay conflict.</p>
  <p>A physical standby also reproduces the primary's physical structures: you cannot add a report-only index or create temporary tables there. If a reporting workload needs different indexes or writable staging tables, this distinction points toward a logical copy or another system. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/hot-standby.html">hot standby documentation</a> describes the restrictions and conflict handling.</p>
 </section>

 <section id="retention"><h2>Replication slots and WAL retention</h2>
  <p>Replay can catch up only if the missing history still exists. PostgreSQL recycles old WAL when it is no longer required; an offline standby might reconnect asking for a segment the primary has already removed. A <strong>replication slot</strong> records a consumer's retention requirement so the primary can keep the needed WAL.</p>
  <p>The physical slot <code>scale_b</code> in our setup belongs to B's stream. Its <code>restart_lsn</code> marks the oldest WAL that might still be needed. As the consumer advances, this boundary can advance too. If B disappears permanently while its slot remains, the primary may retain an ever-growing amount of history.</p>
  <Sql code={monitor} label="On A: inspect replication progress and retained history" />
  <p>The position columns help separate a sender that is behind, a receiver that has not flushed, and replay that has not caught up. The byte difference is a distance in the WAL stream, not an exact disk-usage counter. Inspect slot status, disk headroom and archive health together. The lag time fields in <code>pg_stat_replication</code> describe recent progress delays; they are not a prediction of how many seconds catch-up will take.</p>
  <p>By default, <code>max_slot_wal_keep_size</code> permits unlimited slot retention. Setting a cap can protect space, but when the limit is enforced at a checkpoint the consumer may lose the WAL it needed and require a new copy. Dropping a slot also gives up its retention promise. Neither action repairs the consumer by itself. PostgreSQL's <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-replication.html">replication settings</a> and <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/monitoring-stats.html">monitoring views</a> define the relevant boundaries.</p>
 </section>

 <section id="logical"><h2>Logical replication and replica identity</h2>
  <p>A logical subscriber does not replay the publisher's heap and index pages. Logical decoding turns WAL into changes for published tables; an apply worker performs those changes against the subscriber's own relations. An initial table copy supplies the starting rows, coordinated with subsequent changes so that copying and streaming meet. Because the receiving tables have their own physical storage, they can have different indexes.</p>
  <p>Consider an orders table keyed by <code>(tenant_id, order_id)</code>. Tenant 7 has order 101, worth 25; tenant 8 has order 102, worth 40. We want a reporting copy of tenant 7. The publisher can select those rows:</p>
  <Sql code={publication} label="On the publisher: select tenant 7's orders" />
  <p>The subscriber needs a compatible <code>orders</code> table and a subscription to this publication. The publisher needs logical WAL and the required replication resources and privileges. A publication defines which changes are available; it neither creates the receiving schema nor starts a subscriber by itself.</p>
  <p>For an UPDATE, apply must find the old target row. The primary key supplies the default <strong>replica identity</strong>: here tenant and order together. If the publisher changes order 101 from tenant 7 to tenant 8, the old row matches the filter and the new row does not. The stream represents that transition as a DELETE for this subscriber. Reversing the move produces an INSERT. This follows the <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/logical-replication-row-filter.html">old/new row-filter rules</a>; it is more precise than imagining that every UPDATE is copied unchanged.</p>
  <p>A table without a suitable replica identity cannot simply publish arbitrary updates and deletes. Those source statements can fail. <code>REPLICA IDENTITY FULL</code> can supply the old row as a fallback, but lookup work and data types still matter. PostgreSQL 18 can use eligible subscriber B-tree or hash indexes for this search; FULL does not invariably mean a full scan. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/logical-replication-publication.html">publication reference</a> specifies the conditions.</p>
  <p>We checked that transition on a separate PostgreSQL 18 publisher and subscriber: the initial copy contained only (7, 101, 25); moving order 101 to tenant 8 emptied the subscriber; moving it back restored that row. Adding an index on the subscriber succeeded independently of the publisher.
  </p><p>The independent schema creates maintenance work. Logical replication does not copy DDL or advance the subscriber's sequences. If an identity-generated order number arrives, its value is inserted, but the subscriber's local sequence may still generate that same number later. Before making such a copy a writer, reconcile sequence state and schema as well as row counts. For compatible additive schema changes, updating the subscriber first can avoid an apply failure when new source data arrives. In our check, the replicated order had generated ID 101, but calling the subscriber's own sequence still returned 101. The copied row had not advanced that counter. See the <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/logical-replication-restrictions.html">replication restrictions</a>.</p>
  <p>Ordinary writes on both sides do not become a safe multi-writer system automatically. An incoming change can overwrite a local change; a uniqueness conflict can stop apply; an update whose target is missing can be skipped. Skipping a failing transaction may also skip its valid changes. We deliberately created order 103 locally with total 99, then published the same key with total 12. Apply stopped with a uniqueness conflict; removing our local conflicting row allowed it to continue and insert 12. In a separate step, deleting order 101 locally caused its later source UPDATE to be skipped, while a subsequent source INSERT still arrived. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/logical-replication-conflicts.html">conflict rules</a> are reasons to define who owns each write before enabling local modifications.</p>
 </section>

 <section id="promotion"><h2>Promotion, fencing and rejoining</h2>
  <p>After our synchronous experiment, both servers read price 27. For the next test we removed the synchronous standby requirement, stopped B, and committed price 35 on A. A acknowledged it. Then we stopped A and restarted B without a source to contact. Promoting B made it writable with price 27: it had never received the later change.</p>
  <Diagram kind="failover" />
  <p><code>SELECT pg_promote(true, 10);</code> on B requested promotion and waited for completion. Afterwards <code>pg_is_in_recovery()</code> returned false. That command changes PostgreSQL's role; it does not decide whether A is truly dead, remove A from a load balancer, or break existing client connections to A.</p>
  <p>We had stopped A before promotion, so only one server could accept writes. In a network failure, “I cannot reach A” does not imply “no client can write to A”. A failover controller needs a way to revoke that old authority, such as stopping or isolating the host. PostgreSQL calls this <strong>fencing</strong> in its <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/warm-standby-failover.html">failover guidance</a>. Promotion without it can leave two writable histories.</p>
  <p>Controllers such as Patroni add leader coordination outside core PostgreSQL. A lease in a distributed configuration store identifies the current leader; a watchdog can reset a host whose controller fails to relinquish leadership in time. The lease, reset path and routing arrangement all contribute to the guarantee. <a href="https://patroni.readthedocs.io/en/latest/watchdog.html">Patroni's watchdog documentation</a> explains this failure case rather than treating an updated DNS record as sufficient.</p>
  <p>The stopped A still contained price 35. Simply starting it as a writer would revive the rejected branch. We used <code>pg_rewind</code> to compare the related histories and replace changed blocks with B's state, then configured A to follow B as a standby. It returned price 27 and later received a new price 30 written on B.</p>
  <p>Rewind is not a merge of accepted business operations. The acknowledged price 35 was absent from the surviving branch. If that change represented a payment or another externally visible operation, the application would need to reconcile it from its own durable identities and evidence.</p>
  <p>The first rewind attempt in this experiment failed because the required older WAL had already been recycled. Repeating with enough retained WAL allowed it to finish. The target also needs checksums or suitable <code>wal_log_hints</code> history, with <code>full_page_writes</code> enabled. A new base backup is the fallback when rewind's prerequisites are unavailable. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/app-pgrewind.html">pg_rewind manual</a> covers those requirements and configuring recovery before restarting the target.</p>
  <p>A managed service may own these procedures, but its topology still matters. An <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/Concepts.MultiAZSingleStandby.html">RDS Multi-AZ DB instance</a> has an unavailable-for-reads standby; a <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/multi-az-db-clusters-concepts.html">Multi-AZ DB cluster</a> offers a writer and two readable instances. A service's high-availability label alone does not tell a reader where queries can run or when their results become current.</p>
 </section>

 <section id="logical-failover"><h2>Logical replication across failover</h2>
  <p>For a separate failover scenario, return to the original arrangement: A is the primary and B is its physical standby. Add a logical subscriber C to A. C tracks progress through a logical slot on A. If B is promoted without a corresponding usable slot, knowing that B has the tables does not tell C where to resume its change stream.</p>
  <p>PostgreSQL 18 can synchronize failover-capable logical slots to a standby. This needs deliberate configuration: a failover-enabled logical slot or subscription, slot synchronization on B, B's upstream physical slot and feedback, and the required connection information. Synchronizing slot metadata is asynchronous. Before cutover, the needed slots must be present, persistent and valid on B.</p>
  <p>There is another ordering problem. C could consume a source change before B receives its WAL. Promoting B then asks C to continue from a history that B does not have. The primary's <code>synchronized_standby_slots</code> setting can hold logical sending until specified physical slots confirm receipt, coupling C's forward progress to B's safety. An unavailable required slot can therefore stall C.</p>
  <p>The failover procedure must check both history and slot readiness, fence A, promote B, and repoint the subscriber without continuing to consume from the old writer. This is a coordinated operation, not a guarantee created by one <code>failover=true</code> option. PostgreSQL's <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/logical-replication-failover.html">logical failover procedure</a> and <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/logicaldecoding-explanation.html">slot synchronization explanation</a> provide the exact readiness checks.</p>
  <p>Following a change through these processes tells us which question to ask. A stale page needs replay progress and snapshot context. An expanding WAL directory needs its retention dependencies. A replacement writer needs an authoritative history and a safe route back for the old server. The same copied bytes participate in all three, but each operation depends on a different boundary.</p>
 </section>
